{:edb/db-before-t 1121 :edb/db-after-t 1122 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534434 50 #inst "2026-10-07T03:21:48.392Z" 13194139534434 true] [17592186083207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288042 13194139534434 true] [17592186083207 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288046 13194139534434 true] [17592186083240 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288056 13194139534434 true] [17592186083247 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288035 13194139534434 true] [17592186083247 1020 #edb/ref 17592186288051 13194139534434 true] [17592186288035 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288036 13194139534434 true] [17592186288035 1089 "q-127386" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534434 true] [17592186288035 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ If the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139534434 true] [17592186288035 1113 #uuid "ba7d9686-4512-5ac3-be73-a640ac96e3b1" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288035 1145 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x) \\\\  & =3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} & =\\frac{9}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡3x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡3+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- setting $n=-1$ gives $x≈-0.630\\,85$ to five decimal places\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x≈0.416\\,35$ to five decimal places\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}<x<\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are \n\n$$\nx≈-0.630\\,85,\\,x≈0.416\\,35.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x≈-0.630\\,85$ and $x≈0.416\\,35.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ are ${x}_{1}≈-0.630\\,85$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.416\\,35.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈-0.630\\,85\\cdot 0.416\\,35≈-0.26\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288037 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288038 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288039 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288040 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288041 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1041 "selection" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288040 13194139534434 true] [17592186288036 1110 #uuid "0cb29190-821e-54a5-82ae-f6e53cf766cd" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288037 1034 "-1.13" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288037 1111 #uuid "dd541db5-66dc-504d-b758-616eb5c03e85" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288038 1034 "-0.98" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288038 1111 #uuid "d18150f8-b957-54ba-82cc-6f9ad825b18b" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288039 1034 "-1.43" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288039 1111 #uuid "7f816a31-092d-5ba4-b0d7-ea39d7a68572" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288040 1034 "-0.26" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288040 1111 #uuid "b57c912e-e6be-5bfd-9bb7-860a0def19d0" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288041 1034 "-0.55" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288041 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288041 1111 #uuid "be17fc96-5016-57ea-a7e8-457ed48b8060" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288042 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288043 13194139534434 true] [17592186288042 1089 "q-344608" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288042 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534434 true] [17592186288042 1100 "The critical point of the function $f(x)=(2x+1){e}^{x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." 13194139534434 true] [17592186288042 1113 #uuid "20f9ec3f-e21d-5b61-96bd-c285216e22be" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288042 1145 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1){e}^{x-2}) \\\\  & ={(2x+1)}^{′}{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){({e}^{x-2})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{x-2}+(2x+1){e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =[2+(2x+1)]{e}^{x-2} \\\\  & =(2x+3){e}^{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3){e}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x-2}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{x-2}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a stationary point at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $f(x)$ is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288044 13194139534434 true] [17592186288043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288045 13194139534434 true] [17592186288043 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534434 true] [17592186288043 1041 "field-1" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288043 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288044 13194139534434 true] [17592186288043 1110 #uuid "bfac7c8b-577b-501e-ad6e-be37867afb04" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288044 1034 "-\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288044 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288044 1111 #uuid "0f3858d5-153b-5b2d-8787-d3569adb1d3e" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288045 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288045 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288045 1111 #uuid "c4849e24-9fa1-5c7f-9b87-7c7a6507f213" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288047 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288049 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1089 "q-344842" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1100 "In **ascending order**, the critical points of the function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1113 #uuid "6387fc63-1d7e-52e2-8b83-73d6704377e7" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288046 1145 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{3}-\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{2}+12x)}^{′}{e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3{x}^{2}-15x+12){e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{{x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}/2+12x}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-15x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-5x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-1)(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =1,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=4.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ are  $x=1$ and  $x=4.$" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288048 13194139534434 true] [17592186288047 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534434 true] [17592186288047 1041 "field-1" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288047 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288048 13194139534434 true] [17592186288047 1110 #uuid "9b8383c5-17b3-59eb-bcd4-a81156ab263c" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288048 1034 "1" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288048 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288048 1111 #uuid "31ae11f7-59e6-5463-b97e-35e55e99e07b" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288049 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288050 13194139534434 true] [17592186288049 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534434 true] [17592186288049 1041 "field-2" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288049 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288050 13194139534434 true] [17592186288049 1110 #uuid "13352d2e-9171-5908-9c56-2e91056859af" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288050 1034 "4" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288050 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288050 1111 #uuid "8c97e464-4f7b-5b14-a90c-3bcfc21eb2d3" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288052 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288054 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1089 "q-345484" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ In **ascending order**, the **exact values** of the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1113 #uuid "be26ab5d-93a6-5d26-b3a4-a77f406efe5a" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288051 1145 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi )$ are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288052 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288053 13194139534434 true] [17592186288052 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534434 true] [17592186288052 1041 "field-1" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288052 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288053 13194139534434 true] [17592186288052 1110 #uuid "e73b6396-71c3-5f96-9350-d61717a0c5bd" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288053 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi}{3}" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288053 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288053 1111 #uuid "ac21cd45-3597-51a0-bd6b-82dc40ebe599" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288054 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288055 13194139534434 true] [17592186288054 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534434 true] [17592186288054 1041 "field-2" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288054 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288055 13194139534434 true] [17592186288054 1110 #uuid "cbed6263-0731-57ec-a285-526382133e94" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288055 1034 "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288055 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534434 true] [17592186288055 1111 #uuid "61edb762-254c-5e4e-80bb-81b769c1784f" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288056 1017 #edb/ref 17592186288057 13194139534434 true] [17592186288056 1089 "q-99055" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288056 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534434 true] [17592186288056 1100 "What are the critical points of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}?$" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288056 1113 #uuid "63c773de-f51b-54ba-8cf5-768e0048eb88" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288056 1145 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{′}\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot ({x}^{2}-5)^{′}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)-(x-3)\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5+6x-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+5}{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=1$ and $x=5.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ does not exist at $x=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.$ But these points do not lie in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=5.$" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288058 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288059 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288060 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288061 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1000 #edb/ref 17592186288062 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1041 "selection" 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1104 #edb/ref 17592186288062 13194139534434 true] [17592186288057 1110 #uuid "705be2b5-4a6c-5966-9ba6-952a23c3c